#!/usr/bin/env node
/*
 * tools/githooks/pre-commit -- the pre-commit dispatcher. Check-register row CHK-10.
 * Installed at 2.14 by The Systems Engineer, 2026-08-28.
 * THINNED at 2.20, same author, same day: the engine moved to ./dispatch.js when a SECOND
 * trigger (merge-gate, CHK-38) was installed. Two dispatchers holding two copies of the
 * membership test, the status rule, the argv rule and the exit classification are two
 * authorities on how a check is invoked. What remains here is what is peculiar to this event.
 *
 * CL-8(b) requires this file to name its own row id, so that CHK-10's dispatch join is
 * bidirectional: the register names the hook, and the hook names its row. The literal above,
 * this sentence, and the rowId passed to dispatch() are that.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE IS COMMITTED AND THE HOOK IS NOT. Git hooks are not cloned (loose end E1), so
 * .git/hooks/ is empty on a fresh clone and a hook installed there is a mechanism for exactly
 * one machine. This file lives under version control, and oracle/bootstrap_contract.md BC-8
 * points core.hooksPath at this directory so that a clone acquires the trigger by running the
 * bootstrap. THE CONTRACT IS NOT THE INSTALLATION: BC-8 succeeding is not evidence that this
 * file fires, which is what HK-1 exists to test and why it is asserted at session-start and
 * never from inside a hook (check_register.md section 5.1).
 *
 * THE 100644 TRAP, and it is the reason HK-2 exists. core.filemode is false on the authoring
 * machine, so a hook committed here at mode 100644 is executable in this working tree and INERT
 * on a Linux clone, where it will be skipped in silence and every check it dispatches will be
 * unwired while every check ABOUT the wiring passes. `git ls-files -s tools/githooks/` must
 * report 100755 for every HOOK in this directory. Fix: git update-index --chmod=+x.
 *
 * THE CRLF TRAP IS THE SAME FAMILY AND WAS OPEN UNTIL 2.20. This shebang is LF only because
 * core.autocrlf=true happens to be set on the authoring machine; the repository had no
 * .gitattributes at all, so a clone under a different autocrlf setting checks this file out with
 * CRLF and the kernel reports `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env node`. `tools/githooks/** text
 * eol=lf` in .gitattributes is the fix and it is content rather than per-install configuration,
 * which is the same distinction E12 turns on.
 *
 * EMPTY INDEX. The `git hook run` subcommand invokes hooks with nothing staged, so every check
 * this dispatches must be safe and meaningful with an empty index, and must report an empty scan
 * as a scan of nothing rather than as a pass. That is CHK-13's PDF-16, and it is a property of
 * the checks rather than of this file.
 *
 * The sentence above is deliberately NOT written as the four-word literal CL-8(a) greps for, and
 * the reason is worth a line because it is a weakness in CL-8(a) rather than a courtesy. CL-8(a)
 * is a grep for a string, so IT CANNOT DISTINGUISH AN INVOCATION FROM A MENTION: the files most
 * obliged to explain the recursion are the files the grep hits hardest. CHK-10's invoked_by is
 * `git`, not `pre-commit`, so the clause does not currently reach this file at all -- but it
 * would the moment anyone added `pre-commit` to this row, and it would then fail on a comment.
 * That is the AM-143 shape: a rule that fires on the documentation of the thing it forbids.
 *
 * NO SELF-INVOCATION. This file must never invoke the event it dispatches; that is the
 * CHK-09/CHK-10 recursion measured at the 1.5/1.13 review, which is unbounded because git has no
 * reentrancy guard. The bound now lives in dispatch.js and is SHARED WITH merge-gate, because a
 * per-trigger marker would have bounded each dispatcher inside itself and left
 * pre-commit -> merge-gate -> pre-commit open. CL-8(a) remains the standing grep.
 */

'use strict';

const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const { dispatch } = require('./dispatch.js');

/* HK-3, and it is peculiar to this event, which is why it did not move into the engine. The
 * canary is written before anything is dispatched, so that a bypass is detectable even when a
 * dispatched check would have failed. post-commit (CHK-11) consumes it; when it is absent,
 * --no-verify skipped this hook and post-commit records the bypass. Verified at 1.13:
 * --no-verify skips pre-commit and does NOT skip post-commit. This DETECTS a bypass; nothing
 * here can prevent one, and claiming otherwise would be the more dangerous error. */
function canary(ctx) {
  try {
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(ctx.root, '.git', 'hook-canary'),
      String(Date.now()) + '-' + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2) + '\n');
  } catch (e) {
    ctx.err('could not write .git/hook-canary: ' + e.message + ' (HK-3 bypass detection is blind this commit)');
  }
}

process.exit(dispatch({ trigger: 'pre-commit', rowId: 'CHK-10 pre-commit dispatcher:', before: canary }));
